From: kakaotalk-on-wine
Subject: [PATCH] winewayland: Do not fall back to QWERTY when the locale is
 not the layout's

Ctrl+V pasted from the key labelled V rather than the key that types v,
on a Dvorak keyboard, and every other shortcut was in its QWERTY place
too. The scancode arrives correctly and the driver's tables are right;
they were not being used.

WAYLAND_KbdLayerDescriptor finds a layout by rebuilding its HKL and
comparing, and get_layout_hkl puts the layout's own language in the high
word. The thread that asks is the keyboard event thread, which turns
scancodes into vkeys and has never had a layout activated -- the
activation is a message to the focused window and never reaches it -- so
NtUserGetKeyboardLayout hands it one invented from the user locale,
MAKELONG(locale, locale). Nothing matches that unless the locale happens
to equal the layout's language, and the miss returns NULL, which win32u
answers with its built-in tables. Those are QWERTY.

Which is why this has gone unnoticed: on a QWERTY keyboard the wrong
fallback is right. It shows up as soon as the two differ, and a prefix
whose locale is not the keyboard's language -- a Korean prefix with a US
keyboard, say -- gets it on every key.

Answer with the layout the compositor says is active instead. The driver
knows it; there is no reason to guess from a locale.
---
 dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_keyboard.c
@@ -1013,6 +1013,28 @@ const KBDTABLES *WAYLAND_KbdLayerDescriptor(HKL hkl)
 
     LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(layout, &xkb_layouts, struct layout, entry)
         if (hkl == get_layout_hkl(layout, LOWORD(hkl))) break;
+
+    /* A thread that has never had a layout activated is handed one invented
+     * from the user locale -- MAKELONG(locale, locale) -- and no layout will
+     * ever match that, because get_layout_hkl puts the layout's own language
+     * in the high word. The keyboard event thread is such a thread: the
+     * layout change goes to the focused window by message and never reaches
+     * it, and it is the thread that turns scancodes into vkeys.
+     *
+     * Returning NULL there falls back to the built-in tables, which are
+     * QWERTY. That is invisible on a QWERTY keyboard and wrong on every
+     * other one, and a prefix whose locale differs from the keyboard's
+     * language -- a Korean prefix with a US keyboard, say -- gets it every
+     * time. So answer with the layout the compositor says is in use, which
+     * is what the caller wanted to ask about.
+     */
+    if (&layout->entry == &xkb_layouts && hkl != keyboard_hkl)
+    {
+        WARN("No Xkb layout for HKL %p, falling back to the active one\n", hkl);
+        LIST_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(layout, &xkb_layouts, struct layout, entry)
+            if (keyboard_hkl == get_layout_hkl(layout, LOWORD(keyboard_hkl))) break;
+    }
+
     if (&layout->entry == &xkb_layouts) layout = NULL;
     else xkb_layout_addref(layout);
 
-- 
